A busy morning brings hundreds of reports about the same thing. We keep one ticket per issue
and merge every duplicate into it, so engineering sees one thread with a count.

## The rule

A report is a duplicate when its **verified cause matches** an open ticket.
The [#4471 review](/kb/incidents/4471-review) contradicts the old subject-only
rule in [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) proposes its replacement:

1. resolve the tenant, locale, booking-form version and report channel;
2. inspect the failure mechanism, using the component's classification as evidence;
3. merge into a ticket for the same verified cause, regardless of subject wording;
4. count each report ID once and preserve conflicting or unexplained symptoms.

<Callout>
Matching subjects do not establish matching causes. Pause automatic subject-only
merges while the support desk reviews the replacement decision.
</Callout>

## Try it

<DedupeExplorer />

## When you are unsure

Hold for investigation. Missing tenant information is not evidence of a shared
cause. Request the input, expected and observed dates, locale, form version and
timestamps before merging. Escalate symptoms the classifier does not explain.
